    "snippet": "---\ntitle: National Sports Awards 2024: Manu Bhaker, Harmanpreet Singh and other Indian sporting heroes honoured in New Delhi - full winners list\ndate: 2025-01-02\n---\nNational Sports Awards 2024: Manu Bhaker, Harmanpreet Singh and other Indian sporting heroes honoured in New Delhi - full winners list\n\nManu, Harmanpreet, Gukesh D and Praveen Kumar were presented with the Khel Ratna while 34 athletes received the Arjuna Award at the Rashtrapati Bhavan.\n\nDroupadi Murmu, the President of India, presented the country's standout athletes with the National Sports Awards 2024 at the Rashtrapati Bhavan in New Delhi on Friday.\n\nThe National Sports Awards are given annually by India's Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports to honour standout achievers.\n\nThe Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna, named after the legendary Indian hockey player Dhyan Chand, is India's highest sporting honour followed by the Arjuna Award.\n\nThis year, Olympic medallists Manu Bhaker, Harmanpreet Singh, chess world champion Gukesh D and para-athlete Praveen Kumar were the four recipients of the Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna awards.\n\nManu Bhaker, 22, is an Indian pistol shooter. She became the first Indian woman to win an Olympic medal in shooting with a bronze at the Paris 2024 Olympics in the women's 10m air pistol event.\n\nDays later, Manu teamed up with Sarabjot Singh to win bronze in the 10m air pistol mixed team event, making her the first athlete from independent India to win two medals at a single Olympic Games.\n\nMeanwhile, Harmanpreet Singh led the Indian hockey team to its second consecutive bronze medal at the Olympics at Paris 2024. Regarded as one of the top drag-flickers in modern hockey, Harmanpreet was pivotal in India's campaign, finishing as the tournament's top scorer with 10 goals.\n\nGukesh, 18, was crowned the 2024 world chess champion in Singapore, becoming the youngest world chess champion and only the second Indian after Viswanathan Anand to win the title. He was also part of the men's team that won the Chess Olympiad in Budapest.\n\nPara-athlete Praveen Kumar soared to victory in the men's high jump T64 class with an Asian record at the Paris 2024 Paralymp"
